  "id": "DrugBank_DB09345 - MONDO_1461",
  "verbalization": "Pramocaine shows a clinically relevant indication for Dermatophytosis of Groin and Perianal Area, which is associated with Tinea Corporis. This relationship is supported by the presence of three distinct pathways, all of which link Pramocaine to Tinea Corporis through different antifungal agents—Clioquinol, Naftifine, and Oxiconazole. These agents are known to inhibit fungal growth by disrupting cell membrane integrity or interfering with fungal metabolism, suggesting a shared mechanism of action among the implicated drugs. The high final scores in all three paths indicate a strong, consistent association between Pramocaine and Tinea Corporis, likely due to the shared antifungal properties of the indicated agents. The evidence suggests that Pramocaine may be repurposed for Tinea Corporis, though further mechanistic validation is needed to confirm its therapeutic potential. The integration of these pathways supports the hypothesis that Pramocaine could exert antifungal effects on Tinea Corporis, though the exact mechanism remains to be elucidated.",
